kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The original expression and x + 2 are not equivalent at every real number, because the former is undefined at x = 2 while the latter equals 4 there.","step_ids":["comparison-at-two"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"domain-check","justification":"A quotient is defined only when its denominator is nonzero. Here x - 2 != 0 exactly when x != 2.","statement":"The original expression is defined exactly for real x != 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["domain-check"],"id":"factor-cancel","justification":"x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2). On the domain from domain-check, x - 2 is nonzero, so it may be cancelled.","statement":"For x != 2, (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) = ((x - 2)(x + 2))/(x - 2) = x + 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["domain-check","factor-cancel"],"id":"comparison-at-two","justification":"domain-check excludes x = 2 from the original expression. The polynomial x + 2 is defined at x = 2 and has value 4.","statement":"The simplified formula extends to x = 2, but the original expression does not; hence they are not equal as real-valued expressions on every real number."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
